---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { tree } from '../lib/tree';
---

<Base title="How the numbers are worked out — The Upgrade Tree" description="How an upgrade gets onto the tree, how its stage is decided, how the headline count is worked out, what counts as a record, and what the site does not do.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
    <h1>How the numbers are worked out</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every number on this site is a count of upgrades by stage, so this page is mostly about how
      a stage is decided. Short for now: the rules below are the ones in force on 16 October 2026;
      they grow as the list does, and every change to them is dated on
      <a href="/story/">the story so far</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2>What gets onto the tree</h2>
    <p>
      An upgrade is one specific technology that changes a human being on purpose: a device, a drug,
      a procedure, or a practice such as leaning on an always-on AI assistant. To be written down it
      needs three things: a plain name, a test that says what would count as a person having it
      (written so that two readers would agree), and a source that would settle that test, meaning a
      named public record such as a journal, a regulator or a news organisation. Something that
      cannot be tested that way does not get in.
    </p>

    <h2>The five stages</h2>
    <ol>
      {tree.stages.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.label}.</strong> {s.meaning}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>
      An upgrade sits on the highest rung a public record puts it on. Each rung reached gets a date
      and a link to the record that put it there, and the upgrade's page shows the ladder with those
      dates. Where an earlier rung was reached but the date is not known, the ladder says so rather
      than guessing.
    </p>

    <h2>Three rules that surprise people</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A stage never moves down.</strong> If a device is later withdrawn from sale, the entry
        is marked withdrawn, but the rung it reached stays, because the ladder records what has
        happened. The count on the home page is a count of things that have happened.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>"First human" does not need permission.</strong> A thing done against the rules of
        the field, or the law, still counts as having been done to a person. The 2018 births of
        gene-edited children are on the tree at that rung for exactly this reason.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>There are no odds.</strong> The sister project, The Belt Equation, gives every event
        a probability. This one does not, by a ruling of 16 October 2026: a stage, a date and a record
        say what has happened, and this project does not guess about what will.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The headline count</h2>
    <p>
      The number on the home page is the count of upgrades at <em>first human</em> or above, out of all
      the upgrades on the tree. It is worked out from the record every time the site is built, never
      typed in. The figure beside it shades each branch by the furthest stage reached under it.
    </p>

    <h2>Who does what</h2>
    <p>
      John Fredrickson rules on what goes on the tree and on every stage. Claude, an AI assistant,
      drafts entries, proposes stages and checks records, and built this site. An entry written by
      Claude from memory carries a list of the claims not yet checked against a source, shown on the
      entry's page under "Still to check", and the entry is not treated as settled until that list is
      empty.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not here yet</h2>
    <p>
      A regular reading of the news against every upgrade, so that a stage change is caught within
      days; the second number, how much information can pass between a brain and a computer each
      second, plotted over time on the <a href="/branches/link/">link to machines</a> page; and the
      real list of upgrades, of which the four entries on the tree today are examples.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
